.TH MU2EDAQ-BIGREDBOX-API 7 "2026-10-06" "mu2edaq-bigredbox 2.0.0" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
mu2edaq\-bigredbox\-api \- Big Red Box alert datagram and listener behaviour
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS Transport
An alert is one IPv4 UDP datagram to port 37020
(listener override:
.BR CRS_PORT_UDP ).
The listener binds
.B 0.0.0.0
with
.B SO_BROADCAST
and
.BR SO_REUSEADDR .
All shipped senders default to the limited broadcast address
.BR 255.255.255.255 ,
which reaches every listener on the local segment and never crosses a
router; a listener on another subnet must be sent to by unicast.
Nothing is acknowledged.
.SS Payload
One UTF\-8 JSON object:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
{"system_id": "DAQ\-NODE\-01",
 "timestamp": "2026\-10\-06T14:17:46",
 "message": "Readout buffer overflow"}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B system_id
Who is alerting.
Displayed, and used to choose the spoken template
.RB ( tts_templates.yaml (5)).
.TP
.B timestamp
Sender's local time, no zone offset, seconds; displayed verbatim, never
parsed.
.TP
.B message
Displayed and read aloud in full.
.PP
All keys are optional; a missing or null key is shown as
.B UNKNOWN
or
.BR "(no message text)" ;
a non\-string value is shown as its
.B str()
form.
Extra keys are ignored.
There is no version field and no authentication: any JSON object, including
.BR {} ,
raises an alert.
.PP
The window labels use Qt's automatic text format, so text that Qt judges to be
HTML (for example a
.B <
followed by a tag name such as
.BR b ,
.B p
or
.BR font )
is rendered as rich text and part of it can disappear from the window.
Senders should avoid
.B <
in
.BR message .
.SS Listener processing
.IP 1. 4
Not UTF\-8, not JSON, or JSON that is not an object: logged and dropped.
.IP 2. 4
Any open window has
.B Pause
ticked: logged and dropped, not queued.
.IP 3. 4
Less than 100 ms since the last accepted alert, from any sender: logged and
dropped.
.IP 4. 4
Otherwise: spoken (unless
.B Mute Audio
is ticked) and shown in a new window, or, when two windows are already open,
written over the newest window and appended to its history.
.PP
Every received datagram is logged at INFO, and every dropped one again with
its payload, to
.IR /tmp/daq_alert.log ,
which is not rotated.
.SS Bindings
.TS
l l l l.
Binding	Encoding	Field limit	Bad address
_
C	compact, raw UTF\-8	2041 bytes	sent as broadcast
C++	as C	as C	as C
Python	spaced, \euXXXX	none	exception
.TE
.PP
C is
.BR libdaq_alert (3),
C++ is
.BR libdaq_alert_cpp (3),
Python is
.BR daq_alert (3).
.PP
All bindings put the keys in the order system_id, timestamp, message and
produce datagrams that decode to the same object.
The C and C++ bindings emit identical bytes; the two Python implementations
(the
.B daq_alert
library and
.BR mu2edaq\-bigredbox\-send (1))
emit identical bytes; C and Python differ in separators, the escaping of
backspace, form feed, DEL and non\-ASCII characters.
Any receiver other than a JSON parser must not assume one encoding.
.SS Service discovery
With
.B mu2edaq\-discovery
installed the listener answers DISCOVER requests as
.BR app=bigredbox ,
.BR "name=Big Red Box Alerts" ,
.BR scheme=udp ,
the UDP port,
.BR version=2.0.0 ,
and
.B meta
with string values
.BR version ,
.BR qt ,
.BR pyqt ,
.B python
and
.BR udp_port .
.SS Trust model
Any host that can reach the port can raise an alert window and make the
listener speak arbitrary text.
A sender exceeding ten datagrams per second suppresses alerts from every
other sender through the global rate limit.
The design assumes a closed DAQ network.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS 4
.nf
mu2edaq\-bigredbox\-send \-\-system\-id TEST \-\-message "test, please acknowledge"
echo \-n '{"system_id":"TEST","message":"hi"}' | nc \-u \-w1 127.0.0.1 37020
mu2edaq\-discover \-\-filter app=bigredbox \-\-json
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2edaq\-bigredbox (1),
.BR mu2edaq\-bigredbox\-send (1),
.BR libdaq_alert (3),
.BR libdaq_alert_cpp (3),
.BR daq_alert (3),
